<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Perpetual Edict</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 130 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> · Also known as Edictum perpetuum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Perpetual Edict (Edictum perpetuum) was the fixed, final version of the edict of the Roman praetors, which set out the legal remedies they would grant. Around 130 CE the emperor Hadrian had the jurist Salvius Julianus revise and consolidate it, after which only the emperor could change it. It became a basis of classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a> and of the commentaries of jurists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulpian">Ulpian</a> and Paulus.</p></div>
